commit 9194df6b78adc37a4745f02838e28c9c9023f6f3
Author: burpsy <chaim.shuster@cymotive.com>
Date:   Wed Aug 26 17:52:09 2026 +0300

    fix(matchreplace): an unimplemented target must change nothing, not rewrite the body
    
    `applyOne` implements five of the seven Targets. TargetRequestParamName and
    TargetRequestParamValue have no case, and the default was `return applyBody(r, raw)` —
    so a parameter rule was silently executed as a **whole-body substring rewrite**.
    
    That is not a missing feature behaving conservatively. It is a rule doing something the
    operator did not select, to live traffic, while the API answered 200 and reported it
    compiled.
    
    The proof, now a test:
    
        rule:  {request_param_name, literal, "secret" -> "harmless"}
        in:    user=admin&secret=hunter2&nonsecret=keep
        out:   user=admin&harmless=hunter2&nonharmless=keep
                                           ^^^^^^^^^^
    
    `nonsecret` became `nonharmless`. An unrelated parameter was renamed, because a body
    rewrite has no concept of a parameter and a substring match does not respect field
    boundaries. Both param targets produced byte-identical output, which is itself the
    evidence that neither is implemented — a real applier would treat a name rule and a value
    rule differently.
    
    ## Why this is mine
    
    I widened the accepted rule_type vocabulary in 4469cf1 to include both param targets, and
    justified it in that commit message with "the engine already matches on them
    (matchreplace.go:129)". Line 129 is `targetsDir`, which says which *direction* a target
    belongs to. The applier is `applyOne`, and it had no case for either.
    
    So I verified against one function and asserted about another, and reintroduced the exact
    defect that commit was written to fix — a tolerant default turning an unsupported input
    into a valid-looking wrong answer applied to real requests. Found by an analyst agent that
    proved it behaviourally against a scratch copy of the package rather than by reading, which
    is the only reason it surfaced: `internal/matchreplace` tests passed throughout.
    
    ## The fix
    
    An unimplemented target returns the bytes unchanged. A rule that does nothing shows up in
    the counters as unchanged and can be chased; a rule that quietly rewrites the wrong bytes
    cannot.
    
    `targetsDir` still lists the param targets as request-direction. That remains a true
    statement about which direction they belong to, and it is not a claim that they can be
    applied — conflating those two is what produced this defect, so the comment now says so
    where the next reader will look.
    
    ## Tests
    
    `TestAParamNameRuleDoesNotRewriteUnrelatedParameters` asserts the exact collision above and
    would have failed before this change.
    
    `TestTheImplementedTargetsStillApply` is the guard that matters more: a no-op default is
    correct only for targets with no applier, so without it this change could have quietly
    disabled every real rule and the suite would still have gone green.
    
    The web layer's half — refusing those two rule_types at the API boundary instead of
    accepting them — follows separately. This commit makes the engine safe on its own, so the
    two are independently useful and this one does not depend on the other landing.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>

diff --git a/burpsy-go/internal/matchreplace/matchreplace.go b/burpsy-go/internal/matchreplace/matchreplace.go
